1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,271
These aerospace documentation
demonstrations are built around one

2
00:00:05,321 --> 00:00:07,013
fictional training rig.

3
00:00:07,063 --> 00:00:12,767
Its documentation is an S1000D Issue 6
common source database: data modules,

4
00:00:12,817 --> 00:00:15,694
publication modules and illustrations.

5
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:19,325
The common source database lists every
module.

6
00:00:19,375 --> 00:00:24,019
For each one, it shows what refers to it,
which publications use it,

7
00:00:24,069 --> 00:00:27,106
and what changed from one issue to the
next.

8
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:33,363
In the editor, the form and the XML stay
in step, and the checks run as you type.

9
00:00:33,413 --> 00:00:37,382
A two-digit issue number fails the
identification check.

10
00:00:37,432 --> 00:00:41,379
Put the third digit back, and every layer
passes again.

11
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:46,919
The Simplified Technical English checker
marks each finding in the text,

12
00:00:46,969 --> 00:00:49,383
names its rule, and suggests a fix.

13
00:00:49,433 --> 00:00:54,713
Its word list is an original one, so it
can't certify that a text complies.

14
00:00:55,267 --> 00:01:00,286
The interactive publication shows only
what applies to the serial number you

15
00:01:00,336 --> 00:01:01,336
choose,

16
00:01:00,803 --> 00:01:04,005
and fault isolation asks one question at a
time.

17
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:09,498
In the parts catalogue, select a part in
the exploded view,

18
00:01:09,548 --> 00:01:12,091
and its row in the parts list follows.

19
00:01:14,467 --> 00:01:19,854
Everything here is fictional, and none of
it is maintenance data.

20
00:01:19,904 --> 00:01:24,171
Try it at karandeshmukh.dev/studio slash
aerospace.
